From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aditya kale <252700881+kaleaditya28897-linux@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 13:01:14 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] hurd: no nested functions, part 2: lookups, signals, exec
 (clang has none)

setitimer.c, _Fork.c, hurdexec.c: each nested function a static function
taking what it used, or a macro naming it with the caller's locals.

hurdsig.c: post_signal's helpers static functions given their context;
the sig_post reply a struct hurdsig_reply (port, type, reply RPC) passed
where the nested reply function was, NULL still meaning none
(_hurdsig_abort_rpcs's last parameter, in <hurd/signal.h>; its one other
caller passes NULL).

The lookups: the callbacks take their context.  A struct hurd_lookup_ctx
(sysdeps/hurd/include/hurd/lookup.h, inside libc and ld.so only) holds
use_init_port, get_dtable_port and lookup; an operation on a port is a
struct hurd_port_op.  __hurd_file_name_lookup_c, _split_c,
__hurd_directory_name_split_c, _lookup_retry_c, _path_lookup_c and
__file_name_path_scan_c take them, and libc's own callers (lookup-at.c,
faccessat.c, get-host.c, file_name_lookup_under, ld.so's open_file) use
them.  The exported functions keep their ABI: they call the _c forms
with their callbacks as a context, whose use_init_port gives the
caller's a plain operate that finds its operation through a thread's
pointer, each call keeping the one it replaces (none when the caller's
is _hurd_ports_use).  Behaviour is unchanged, upstream's quirks included
(the tty retry reauthenticates into the lookup's result).

Joist, Phase 5 step 7.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
